{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_sL8pdPenZAUmyAz6QX4UCLqz","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\nIntervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access an Intervals.icu user’s data. It allows users to grant and revoke API access on a per-application basis and keeps user authentication details safe.\n\n## Heading link Creating An Application\n\nFill in the application form here: https://intervals.icu/oauth/apply\n\nYou need to be logged in as the Intervals.icu athlete that will own the app. This athlete will have access to the management page to edit the app, setup redirect URI’s, webhooks and other things.\n\nYour app will show up on the /settings/apps page (only for you as the owner) and you can click “Manage App” to retrieve your client\\_id and secret, change redirect URL’s, configure webhooks and so on.\n\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/cb935eaa9a1b9e784347f5a9486fe24a.png)\n\nThe app will have status “Pending” until it is approved. **Please note that you will not be able to do the OAuth flow until it is approved**. You can upload a logo, get the client ID and secret and so on.\n\nOnce it has been approved, the pending tag will disappear. You will receive a system notification when this happens (delivered by email and in the Intervals.icu app).\n\nNote that it will not be visible to other users in /settings/apps, but you can send anyone you like to the OAuth consent page and begin integration.\n\n\n![image2](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/ef7703e10049bf07ae1b507e4796aacc.png)\n\nWhen you have completed your integration and tested properly, contact support@intervals.icu to have it listed in the public app directory. Please include a link to the management page in the email.\n\n## Heading link Requesting Authorization To Access Intervals.icu Data For A User\n\nSend the user to:\n\n```php-template hljs\nhttps://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=<your client id>\n    &redirect_uri=<your redirect uri>\n    &scope=<required scopes>\n    &state=<optional data>\n\n```\n\nIntervals.icu will ask the user to login and display a confirmation dialog with options to choose which\n\nscopes to grant the application. If the user confirms then they are redirected to the redirect\\_uri with an\n\nauthorization code and the optional state parameter:\n\n```perl hljs\n<your redirect uri>?code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4&state=...\n\n```\n\nIf they decline:\n\n```go hljs\n<your redirect uri>?error=access_denied\n\n```\n\nYour server needs to exchange the code for an access token within 2 minutes by POSTing form data including your client\\_id and client\\_secret:\n\n```cpp hljs\ncurl -X POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token \\\n    -d client_id=... \\\n    -d client_secret=... \\\n    -d code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4\n\n```\n\nIf all goes well Intervals.icu will respond with an access token, granted scopes and the id and name of the athlete:\n\n```json hljs\n{\n    \"token_type\": \"Bearer\",\n    \"access_token\": \"d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9\",\n    \"scope\": \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:WRITE\",\n    \"athlete\": {\n        \"id\": \"2049151\",\n        \"name\": \"David (intervals.icu)\"\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\nTo call the API use “Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9” header.\n\nNote that you can use “0” for the athlete id for endpoints that accept an athlete id in the path. This will use the athlete for the bearer token used to make the call.\n\n### Heading link PKCE (optional)\n\nPKCE is supported to protect the authorization code from interception. Generate a random `code_verifier` (43-128 chars of `A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~`) and add its challenge to the authorize URL (only `S256` is supported):\n\n```\n    &code_challenge=<BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier)) without padding>\n    &code_challenge_method=S256\n\n```\n\nThen include `-d code_verifier=...` when exchanging the code. If a challenge was sent, the verifier is required, and a verifier is rejected if no challenge was sent. The client\\_secret is still required unless your app is a public client.\n\nOnce every version of your app uses PKCE, tick “Require PKCE” on your app’s settings page. Authorizations without a code\\_challenge are then rejected, and so is exchanging a code without a code\\_verifier, even with the client\\_secret.\n\n### Heading link Public clients (SPA, mobile and desktop apps)\n\nApps that cannot keep a client\\_secret confidential can be flagged as public clients on the OAuth app management page. Public clients that use PKCE can omit client\\_secret when exchanging the code. Without PKCE the client\\_secret is still required (this is supported so existing apps can migrate, please switch to PKCE). The automatic http://localhost redirect exception does not apply to public clients, so register the exact redirect URIs you use, including any localhost ones. The port of a registered http://localhost or http://127.0.0.1 redirect is ignored, so native apps can listen on any free port:\n\n```\ncurl -X POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token \\\n    -d client_id=...\n    -d code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4\n    -d code_verifier=...\n\n```\n\n## Heading link Scopes\n\nScopes are as follows:\n\n- ACTIVITY: Completed rides, runs etc.\n- WELLNESS: Weight, resting HR etc.\n- CALENDAR: Planned workouts\n- CHATS: Chats, groups and messages\n- LIBRARY: Workout library\n- SETTINGS: Athlete settings\n\nFor each scope specify READ or WRITE (to update, implies READ access) and use commas to separate multiple scopes. Example:\n\n```ruby hljs\nACTIVITY:READ,WELLNESS:WRITE\n\n```\n\nRequests read access to activities and read and write access to wellness data.\n\n## Heading link Revoking An Access Token\n\nWhen a user disconnects Intervals.icu in your app or deletes their data on your side, revoke the access token:\n\n```sql hljs\ncurl -X DELETE \"https://intervals.icu/api/v1/disconnect-app\" \\\n -H 'authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThis will stop webhook delivery for the athlete.\n\n## Heading link Your Own Data\n\nNote that you don’t need to do all this if you just want access to your own data. Use your API key to do that.\n\n## Heading link API Endpoints"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_a4Q4JI1zA5mvHOfIZYe3NxkP","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\n## post by johannesh83 on 5 Jun\n\nGlad to hear, I assume the better the input is prepared for you to fix, change, the fast the change is there. Thanks David, you really live my dream of being a “fast-delivering-quality-codeworker”!\n\n19 days later\n\n\n## post by david on 24 Jun\n\nWe are going to be adding per OAuth client (app) and per athlete (API key) rate limits soon. The goal is to protect the infrastructure from bugs in apps and scripts, not to reduce access. An app with a lot of users that suddenly starts spamming requests can cause problems. Details will appear on the first post in this thread soon.\n\nThe current rate limits (10 calls/second per IP address) will remain in place. This is applied before the request reaches the servers.\n\n## post by david on 25 Jun\n\n## Heading link Rate Limits\n\n(draft, this is not live yet, please shout if this isn’t going to work for you or you see other problems)\n\nThere are limits per day (reset at midnight UTC) and per rolling 15 minute window. The following http headers are returned on each request:\n\n```bash hljs\nX-RateLimit-Limit: <15m limit>,<daily limit>\nX-RateLimit-Remaining: <15m limit remaining>,<daily limit remaining>\n\n```\n\nOver rate limit requests receive a 429 status code and a Retry-After http header indicating how many seconds need to elapse until the call might succeed.\n\n```makefile hljs\nRetry-After: 370\n\n```\n\nThere is an additional limit of 10 calls per second per IP address. This limit does not return any rate limit related http headers.\n\nRate limits are enforced differently for API key callers and OAuth client apps.\n\n### Heading link OAuth Client Apps\n\nThe default daily rate limit is 100/user per day up to 500 users (max 50000 requests), with a minimum of 5000. The daily limit resets at midnight UTC.\n\nThe requests per 15 minute limit is 1/8 of the daily limit with a minimum of 2500. This is a rolling limit and does not reset at specific intervals.\n\nIf you have more than 500 users and need a higher daily limit, or a lower limit for safety, contact support@intervals.icu. The limits are to protect the Intervals.icu infrastructure from apps with many users suddenly generating many more calls than usual.\n\nCurrent limits and API usage information can be found on the oauth client management page (look for “Manage App” on your app on /settings/apps).\n\n### Heading link API Key Callers\n\nAPI key calls are limited to 5000 requests per day and 2500 requests per rolling 15 minute window. If you need more than that you need to create an app using OAuth.\n\n## post by Ajzok on 25 Jun\n\nHello! I tried to use APIs in the Intervals.icu API docs . But I always get an error:\n\n\"API Server\n\nAuthentication\n\nRequired (None Applied)\n\nResponse Status: Failed to fetch (CORS or Network Issue)\"\n\nSorry, I’m doing something wrong and I’m stuck.\n\nFirst of all, I entered in the top right “API\\_KEY” (without quotes) as username and my API KEY from intervals as password. Then I chose, for example, a section\n\n\"\n\nGet athlete profile info\n\nget /api/v1/athlete/{id}/profile\n\n\"\n\nAnd in the ID field I entered my ID-number (from intervals) without i.\n\n## post by MedTechCD on 25 Jun\n\nYou must include the i in the athlete id.\n\n## post by R2Tom on 25 Jun\n\nIf you are trying this on these pages, this is currently not working, because they are configured as http. You need https.\n\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/5954f1630bd976c197c4dc92b261818a.png)\n\nNothing you can do about it at the moment.\n\nHopefully some admin will fix this soon @david @eva @ryandev\n\nBut you can use the curl in a terminal and replace the http with https.\n\nDon‘t foget the `i` as athlete id, if your id has an i, like @MedTechCD said.\n\n## post by david on 25 Jun\n\nThis is fixed for deployment Friday AM (GMT+2). Sorry that took so long to sort out.\n\n## post by Gerald on 26 Jun\n\nJust an FYI, the “i” in athleteID won’t work for some of the very first users. All my scripts have a fallback built in as mine works without the i.\n\n## post by david on 26 Jun\n\nLong ago, when Intervals.icu was Strava only, it used Strava athlete IDs (no i prefix). The prefix was added to avoid collisions when Intervals.icu switched to using its own IDs for athletes.\n\n## post by andrewramsay on 4 Jul\n\nHi, just wanted to start by saying thanks for allowing API access, it’s been really useful for me!\n\nI have a custom local dashboard that I use to display/explore my own data. Currently I’m retrieving my new activities from the intervals API using either a manual trigger or a check for newly uploaded activities once every 30 mins. I’d really like to replace these methods with the `ACTIVITY_UPLOADED` webhook, but I believe this is only possible for OAuth apps at the moment.\n\nSo two questions I guess:\n\n- Am I correct in thinking it’s not possible to use webhooks with the REST API?\n- Assuming that’s the case, is it OK to for me to register an OAuth app even if it’s purely for my own use, just so I can take advantage of the webhook functionality?\n\n## post by Crazy\\_Pete on 6 Jul\n\nA couple of questions..\n\n- Do OAuth access tokens expire? If so, what’s the lifetime?\n- Is there a refresh-token flow, or are access tokens long-lived until the user revokes access?\n- If a token stops working, is the correct recovery simply to send the user back through /oauth/authorize again?\n- For revocation detection on a long-lived server integration - does the APP\\_SCOPE\\_CHANGED webhook fire when a user removes or changes the app’s permissions? And/or does the API return a specific error code on calls made with a revoked token?\n\nThanks.\n\n## post by R2Tom on 6 Jul\n\nNo\n\nNo\n\nYes\n\nYes.\n\nI’m not sure in this, I think it was access denied error.\n\n18 days later\n\n\n## post by david on 24 Jul\n\nWe just added a new endpoint:\n\n**GET** /api/v1/athlete/{id}/connections\n\nThis returns all the devices and platforms the athlete has connected, for better onboarding of new users.\n\n```json hljs\n{\n    \"id\": \"2049151\",\n    \"garmin_health_connected\": true,\n    \"garmin_training_connected\": true,\n    \"polar_connected\": true,\n    \"suunto_connected\": true,\n    \"coros_connected\": true,\n    \"concept2_connected\": false,\n    \"zepp_connected\": false,\n    \"huawei_connected\": true,\n    \"wahoo_connected\": true,\n    \"zwift_connected\": true,\n    \"oura_connected\": false,\n    \"whoop_connected\": true,\n    \"strava_connected\": true,\n    \"dropbox_connected\": true,\n    \"hammerhead_connected\": false,\n    \"tp_virtual_connected\": false,\n    \"rouvy_connected\": false,\n    \"mywhoosh_connected\": true,\n    \"biketerra_connected\": true,\n    \"tymewear_connected\": true\n}\n\n```\n\n## post by david on 30 Jul\n\n## post by Robin\\_Curbelo on 1 Aug\n\n13 days later\n\n\n## post by b3nschiii on 14 Aug\n\n## post by MedTechCD on 14 Aug\n\n## post by R2Tom on 14 Aug\n\n## post by b3nschiii on 18 Aug\n\n17 days later\n\n\n## post by DiogoRM on 5 Sep"}
